{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts'","aggregated_output":"     1\t// Durable work-item mechanics: recording, ordered listing, and the enqueue\n     2\t// wrapper that makes \"queueing is what schedules processing\" structural\n     3\t// (DD-5). The util is domain-blind by the tech-arch capability rule — it\n     4\t// knows the item vocabulary (owner, kind, sourceRef) but nothing about what\n     5\t// a turn or a summary is; which kinds queue for which sources, and which\n     6\t// derivations a kind produces, is the owning domains' business (callers\n     7\t// pass the derivation targets in). Writes run on the caller's handle inside the\n     8\t// ambient transaction, so items commit (or roll back) with the batch. No\n     9\t// public SDK surface: read-back reaches here only through the owning\n    10\t// domains' listQueuedWork.\n    11\timport type { DatabaseSync } from \"node:sqlite\";\n    12\timport {\n    13\t  createCommitHooks,\n    14\t  type OperationContext,\n    15\t} from \"../../shared/context.js\";\n    16\timport type {\n    17\t  CompletionTx,\n    18\t  DerivationType,\n    19\t  HandlerDerivationWrite,\n    20\t  SubjectKind,\n    21\t} from \"../../shared/derivation.js\";\n    22\t\n    23\texport type WorkOwner = \"messages\" | \"turns\";\n    24\texport type WorkKind =\n    25\t  | \"prompt_smoothing\"\n    26\t  | \"tool_call_summary\"\n    27\t  | \"tool_result_summary\"\n    28\t  | \"turn_derivation\"\n    29\t  | \"chunk_summary_detailed\"\n    30\t  | \"chunk_summary_brief\";\n    31\texport type WorkSourceRef = { messageId: string } | { turnId: string } | { chunkId: string };\n    32\t\n    33\t// The work-kind registry: owner and sourceRef semantics per the epic's Work\n    34\t// Item contract. Mechanical metadata only — what a kind *means* stays with\n    35\t// the owning domain's handler (registered at SDK construction, DD-6).\n    36\texport const WORK_KIND_REGISTRY: Readonly<\n    37\t  Record<WorkKind, { owner: WorkOwner; sourceRefKey: \"messageId\" | \"turnId\" | \"chunkId\" }>\n    38\t> = {\n    39\t  prompt_smoothing: { owner: \"messages\", sourceRefKey: \"messageId\" },\n    40\t  tool_call_summary: { owner: \"messages\", sourceRefKey: \"messageId\" },\n    41\t  tool_result_summary: { owner: \"messages\", sourceRefKey: \"messageId\" },\n    42\t  turn_derivation: { owner: \"turns\", sourceRefKey: \"turnId\" },\n    43\t  chunk_summary_detailed: { owner: \"turns\", sourceRefKey: \"chunkId\" },\n    44\t  chunk_summary_brief: { owner: \"turns\", sourceRefKey: \"chunkId\" },\n    45\t};\n    46\t\n    47\texport interface WorkItemRecord {\n    48\t  workItemId: string;\n    49\t  owner: WorkOwner;\n    50\t  kind: WorkKind;\n    51\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\n    52\t  status: \"queued\"; // the only status written before a drain claims (Epic 02 Story 1)\n    53\t  queuedAt: string;\n    54\t}\n    55\t\n    56\t// The derivations an enqueue is scheduling work toward; the owning domain\n    57\t// names them (the util stays meaning-blind). Stored in the row's payload so\n    58\t// the drain's terminal paths (retry exhaustion, blocked source) can land the\n    59\t// derivation's failed/blocked state without asking any domain what a kind means.\n    60\texport interface EnqueueDerivationTarget {\n    61\t  subjectKind: SubjectKind;\n    62\t  subjectId: string;\n    63\t  derivationType: DerivationType;\n    64\t}\n    65\t\n    66\texport interface WorkItemInput {\n    67\t  owner: WorkOwner;\n    68\t  kind: WorkKind;\n    69\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\n    70\t  sourceVersion?: number; // defaults to 1 — first version of a fresh source\n    71\t  derivations?: readonly EnqueueDerivationTarget[]; // carried in payload for the drain's terminal paths\n    72\t}\n    73\t\n    74\tfunction sourceIdOf(sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): string {\n    75\t  if (\"messageId\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.messageId;\n    76\t  if (\"turnId\" in sourceRef) return sourceRef.turnId;\n    77\t  return sourceRef.chunkId;\n    78\t}\n    79\t\n    80\t// Deterministic id scoped to the source version (DD-1/DD-3): re-queueing the\n    81\t// same kind for the same source *at the same version* is the same id —\n    82\t// natural idempotency for the repair path — while a post-mutation\n    83\t// replacement (next version) never collides with an in-flight pre-mutation\n    84\t// item.\n    85\texport function recordItem(\n    86\t  db: DatabaseSync,\n    87\t  input: WorkItemInput,\n    88\t  queuedAt: string,\n    89\t): WorkItemRecord {\n    90\t  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\n    91\t  const workItemId = `w-${sourceIdOf(input.sourceRef)}-${input.kind}-v${sourceVersion}`;\n    92\t  db.prepare(\n    93\t    `INSERT INTO work_item (work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, queued_at, payload)\n    94\t     VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, 'queued', ?, ?)`,\n    95\t  ).run(\n    96\t    workItemId,\n    97\t    input.owner,\n    98\t    input.kind,\n    99\t    JSON.stringify(input.sourceRef),\n   100\t    queuedAt,\n   101\t    JSON.stringify({ sourceVersion, derivations: input.derivations ?? [] }),\n   102\t  );\n   103\t  return {\n   104\t    workItemId,\n   105\t    owner: input.owner,\n   106\t    kind: input.kind,\n   107\t    sourceRef: input.sourceRef,\n   108\t    status: \"queued\",\n   109\t    queuedAt,\n   110\t  };\n   111\t}\n   112\t\n   113\texport interface EnqueueInput extends WorkItemInput {\n   114\t  derivations: readonly EnqueueDerivationTarget[];\n   115\t}\n   116\t\n   117\t// The one way work is scheduled (DD-5): the work row, the derivation's `pending`\n   118\t// state row, and the scheduler poke all ride the ambient transaction — they\n   119\t// commit together or vanish together. Enqueue is the *only* place a\n   120\t// derivation row is created (completion is UPDATE-only); re-enqueueing\n   121\t// resets an existing row to pending at the enqueued source version.\n   122\texport function enqueue(ctx: OperationContext, input: EnqueueInput): WorkItemRecord {\n   123\t  const item = recordItem(ctx.db, input, ctx.clock().toISOString());\n   124\t  const sourceVersion = input.sourceVersion ?? 1;\n   125\t  const upsert = ctx.db.prepare(\n   126\t    `INSERT INTO derivation (subject_kind, subject_id, derivation_type, state, source_version)\n   127\t     VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'pending', ?)\n   128\t     ON CONFLICT (subject_kind, subject_id, derivation_type) DO UPDATE SET\n   129\t       state = 'pending', content = NULL, reason = NULL, metadata = NULL,\n   130\t       gaps = NULL, derived_at = NULL, source_version = excluded.source_version`,\n   131\t  );\n   132\t  for (const target of input.derivations) {\n   133\t    upsert.run(target.subjectKind, target.subjectId, target.derivationType, sourceVersion);\n   134\t  }\n   135\t  ctx.onCommit(() => ctx.poke(ctx.threadId));\n   136\t  return item;\n   137\t}\n   138\t\n   139\tinterface RawWorkItemRow {\n   140\t  work_item_id: string;\n   141\t  owner: string;\n   142\t  kind: string;\n   143\t  source_ref: string;\n   144\t  queued_at: string;\n   145\t}\n   146\t\n   147\t// ── claim mechanics (Epic 02 Story 1, tech design §Mechanics) ─────\n   148\t// All pure SQL against the caller's open handle, domain-blind. Claim,\n   149\t// complete, and the terminal half of failAttempt each own one short\n   150\t// BEGIN IMMEDIATE; the handler in between runs with no open transaction.\n   151\t\n   152\t// The claimed row as the drain holds it: mechanical fields plus the payload's\n   153\t// source version and form targets. `kind` is a plain string here — a raw row\n   154\t// with an unregistered kind must still be claimable so the drain can land it\n   155\t// failed_terminal instead of skipping it (AC-1.8).\n   156\texport interface ClaimedWorkItem {\n   157\t  workItemId: string;\n   158\t  owner: WorkOwner;\n   159\t  kind: string;\n   160\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\n   161\t  attempts: number;\n   162\t  queuedAt: string;\n   163\t  sourceVersion: number;\n   164\t  derivations: EnqueueDerivationTarget[];\n   165\t}\n   166\t\n   167\texport type ClaimOutcome =\n   168\t  | { outcome: \"claimed\"; item: ClaimedWorkItem }\n   169\t  | { outcome: \"empty\" }\n   170\t  | { outcome: \"in_flight\"; claimExpiresAt: string }\n   171\t  | { outcome: \"waiting\"; waitingUntil: string };\n   172\t\n   173\tinterface RawClaimRow {\n   174\t  work_item_id: string;\n   175\t  owner: string;\n   176\t  kind: string;\n   177\t  source_ref: string;\n   178\t  attempts: number | bigint;\n   179\t  queued_at: string;\n   180\t  payload: string | null;\n   181\t}\n   182\t\n   183\t// Form targets for pre-v5 rows, whose payload is NULL (Epic 01 wrote no\n   184\t// payload column). Exactly the F-02 backfill's kind→form mapping (migration\n   185\t// v5, shared/storage.ts): the backfill created pending derivation rows at\n   186\t// source version 1 for these kinds, and a terminal failure must find them —\n   187\t// otherwise exhaustion would delete the work row and strand the backfilled\n   188\t// form as pending forever, breaking AC-1.9/DD-1 for upgraded threads.\n   189\tconst LEGACY_KIND_DERIVATIONS: Partial<\n   190\t  Record<string, ReadonlyArray<{ subjectKind: SubjectKind; derivationType: DerivationType }>>\n   191\t> = {\n   192\t  prompt_smoothing: [{ subjectKind: \"message\", derivationType: \"smoothed_prompt\" }],\n   193\t  tool_result_summary: [{ subjectKind: \"message\", derivationType: \"tool_result_summary\" }],\n   194\t  turn_derivation: [\n   195\t    { subjectKind: \"turn\", derivationType: \"turn_rendering\" },\n   196\t    { subjectKind: \"turn\", derivationType: \"lower_band_projection\" },\n   197\t  ],\n   198\t};\n   199\t\n   200\tfunction legacyDerivationTargets(kind: string, sourceRef: WorkSourceRef): EnqueueDerivationTarget[] {\n   201\t  const mapped = LEGACY_KIND_DERIVATIONS[kind];\n   202\t  if (mapped === undefined) return [];\n   203\t  const subjectId = sourceIdOf(sourceRef);\n   204\t  return mapped.map((target) => ({ ...target, subjectId }));\n   205\t}\n   206\t\n   207\tfunction toClaimedItem(row: RawClaimRow): ClaimedWorkItem {\n   208\t  const sourceRef = JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef;\n   209\t  const payload =\n   210\t    row.payload === null\n   211\t      ? // Pre-v5 row: version 1 (matching the backfill) and derivation targets\n   212\t        // reconstructed from the backfill's own mapping.\n   213\t        { sourceVersion: 1, derivations: legacyDerivationTargets(row.kind, sourceRef) }\n   214\t      : (JSON.parse(row.payload) as { sourceVersion?: number; derivations?: EnqueueDerivationTarget[] });\n   215\t  return {\n   216\t    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\n   217\t    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\n   218\t    kind: row.kind,\n   219\t    sourceRef,\n   220\t    attempts: Number(row.attempts),\n   221\t    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\n   222\t    sourceVersion: payload.sourceVersion ?? 1,\n   223\t    derivations: payload.derivations ?? [],\n   224\t  };\n   225\t}\n   226\t\n   227\t// Head-first, never skip-ahead: the claim decision is made against the oldest\n   228\t// live row only — a later eligible row is never considered while an older\n   229\t// live row exists. One atomic UPDATE under BEGIN IMMEDIATE (no read-then-\n   230\t// write split: the race window is the bug class). The CASE increments\n   231\t// `attempts` only when reclaiming an expired claimed row — a reclaim means a\n   232\t// run died or hung, and the increment makes the crash visible in the report\n   233\t// and counts it against the retry budget; normal claims never touch it.\n   234\texport function claimNext(db: DatabaseSync, now: string, leaseDurationMs: number): ClaimOutcome {\n   235\t  const expiresAt = new Date(Date.parse(now) + leaseDurationMs).toISOString();\n   236\t  db.exec(\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\");\n   237\t  try {\n   238\t    const claimed = db\n   239\t      .prepare(\n   240\t        `UPDATE work_item SET status = 'claimed', claimed_at = ?, claim_expires_at = ?,\n   241\t           attempts = attempts + CASE WHEN status = 'claimed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END\n   242\t         WHERE work_item_id = (\n   243\t           SELECT work_item_id FROM (\n   244\t             SELECT work_item_id, status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\n   245\t             FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\n   246\t             ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1\n   247\t           )\n   248\t           WHERE (status = 'queued' AND (eligible_at IS NULL OR eligible_at <= ?))\n   249\t              OR (status = 'claimed' AND claim_expires_at <= ?)\n   250\t         )\n   251\t         RETURNING work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, attempts, queued_at, payload`,\n   252\t      )\n   253\t      .get(now, expiresAt, now, now) as unknown as RawClaimRow | undefined;\n   254\t    if (claimed !== undefined) {\n   255\t      db.exec(\"COMMIT;\");\n   256\t      return { outcome: \"claimed\", item: toClaimedItem(claimed) };\n   257\t    }\n   258\t    // No row claimable: re-read the head inside the same transaction to\n   259\t    // classify the stop. A claimed head with a live lease gates the queue\n   260\t    // (in_flight, AC-1.4); a queued head still backing off gates it too\n   261\t    // (waiting) — strict ordering, never reordered around the head.\n   262\t    const head = db\n   263\t      .prepare(\n   264\t        `SELECT status, eligible_at, claim_expires_at\n   265\t         FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')\n   266\t         ORDER BY rowid LIMIT 1`,\n   267\t      )\n   268\t      .get() as unknown as\n   269\t      | { status: string; eligible_at: string | null; claim_expires_at: string | null }\n   270\t      | undefined;\n   271\t    db.exec(\"COMMIT;\");\n   272\t    if (head === undefined) return { outcome: \"empty\" };\n   273\t    if (head.status === \"claimed\") {\n   274\t      return { outcome: \"in_flight\", claimExpiresAt: head.claim_expires_at ?? \"\" };\n   275\t    }\n   276\t    return { outcome: \"waiting\", waitingUntil: head.eligible_at ?? \"\" };\n   277\t  } catch (cause) {\n   278\t    db.exec(\"ROLLBACK;\");\n   279\t    throw cause;\n   280\t  }\n   281\t}\n   282\t\n   283\t// Completion: the version-checked derivation writes and the item row's deletion in\n   284\t// one short transaction (DD-1: terminal transition deletes the row; the\n   285\t// disposition is reported in-memory, never stored). UPDATE-only, never\n   286\t// upsert — the pending row exists from enqueue; a write that finds no row at\n   287\t// the item's source version is discarded as stale (DD-3 truth table). The\n   288\t// optional onApplied hook (Story 3) runs inside this same transaction, after\n   289\t// the derivation writes and only when they landed — chunk placement and the\n   290\t// close→summary enqueues ride the completion commit; its onCommit\n   291\t// registrations (the enqueue pokes) flush after COMMIT and drop on rollback.\n   292\texport function complete(\n   293\t  db: DatabaseSync,\n   294\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\n   295\t  writes: readonly HandlerDerivationWrite[],\n   296\t  derivedAt: string,\n   297\t  onApplied?: (tx: CompletionTx) => void,\n   298\t): \"done\" | \"stale_discarded\" {\n   299\t  const hooks = createCommitHooks();\n   300\t  db.exec(\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\");\n   301\t  try {\n   302\t    let hits = 0;\n   303\t    const update = db.prepare(\n   304\t      `UPDATE derivation\n   305\t       SET state = 'ready', content = ?, reason = NULL, metadata = ?, gaps = ?, derived_at = ?\n   306\t       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND derivation_type = ? AND source_version = ?`,\n   307\t    );\n   308\t    for (const write of writes) {\n   309\t      const changed = update.run(\n   310\t        write.content,\n   311\t        write.metadata === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.metadata),\n   312\t        write.gaps === undefined ? null : JSON.stringify(write.gaps),\n   313\t        derivedAt,\n   314\t        write.subjectKind,\n   315\t        write.subjectId,\n   316\t        write.derivationType,\n   317\t        item.sourceVersion,\n   318\t      );\n   319\t      hits += Number(changed.changes);\n   320\t    }\n   321\t    const stale = writes.length > 0 && hits === 0;\n   322\t    if (!stale && onApplied !== undefined) {\n   323\t      onApplied({ db, onCommit: hooks.register });\n   324\t    }\n   325\t    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\n   326\t    db.exec(\"COMMIT;\");\n   327\t    hooks.flush();\n   328\t    return stale ? \"stale_discarded\" : \"done\";\n   329\t  } catch (cause) {\n   330\t    db.exec(\"ROLLBACK;\");\n   331\t    throw cause;\n   332\t  }\n   333\t}\n   334\t\n   335\texport interface RetryPolicy {\n   336\t  budget: number;\n   337\t  backoffBaseMs: number;\n   338\t  backoffCapMs: number;\n   339\t}\n   340\t\n   341\texport type FailAttemptResult =\n   342\t  | { terminal: false; attempts: number; eligibleAt: string }\n   343\t  | { terminal: true; attempts: number };\n   344\t\n   345\t// Terminal failure: the derivation rows land `failed` (or `blocked` for source\n   346\t// damage) carrying the final reason plus attempts/last-error copied onto the\n   347\t// derivation's metadata, and the item row is deleted — one transaction (DD-1).\n   348\t// Derivation writes stay version-checked: if a mutation moved the source since this\n   349\t// item was queued, the stale failure must not stamp the rebuilt derivation.\n   350\texport function failTerminal(\n   351\t  db: DatabaseSync,\n   352\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\n   353\t  opts: { reason: string; formState: \"failed\" | \"blocked\"; attempts: number; now: string },\n   354\t): void {\n   355\t  db.exec(\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\");\n   356\t  try {\n   357\t    const update = db.prepare(\n   358\t      `UPDATE derivation\n   359\t       SET state = ?, content = NULL, reason = ?, metadata = ?, gaps = NULL, derived_at = ?\n   360\t       WHERE subject_kind = ? AND subject_id = ? AND derivation_type = ? AND source_version = ?`,\n   361\t    );\n   362\t    const metadata = JSON.stringify({ attempts: opts.attempts, lastError: opts.reason });\n   363\t    for (const target of item.derivations) {\n   364\t      update.run(\n   365\t        opts.formState,\n   366\t        opts.reason,\n   367\t        metadata,\n   368\t        opts.now,\n   369\t        target.subjectKind,\n   370\t        target.subjectId,\n   371\t        target.derivationType,\n   372\t        item.sourceVersion,\n   373\t      );\n   374\t    }\n   375\t    db.prepare(`DELETE FROM work_item WHERE work_item_id = ?`).run(item.workItemId);\n   376\t    db.exec(\"COMMIT;\");\n   377\t  } catch (cause) {\n   378\t    db.exec(\"ROLLBACK;\");\n   379\t    throw cause;\n   380\t  }\n   381\t}\n   382\t\n   383\t// A failed run counts itself. Under budget and retryable: the row goes back\n   384\t// to `queued` with attempts incremented and eligibility pushed out by backoff\n   385\t// (min(base × 2^attempts, cap)) — retry is not a status (DD-1). At budget, or\n   386\t// on a non-retryable failure, the item is terminal: derivation `failed` with the\n   387\t// final reason, row deleted (AC-1.9).\n   388\texport function failAttempt(\n   389\t  db: DatabaseSync,\n   390\t  item: ClaimedWorkItem,\n   391\t  opts: { reason: string; retryable: boolean; now: string; retry: RetryPolicy },\n   392\t): FailAttemptResult {\n   393\t  const attempts = item.attempts + 1;\n   394\t  if (opts.retryable && attempts < opts.retry.budget) {\n   395\t    const backoffMs = Math.min(\n   396\t      opts.retry.backoffBaseMs * 2 ** attempts,\n   397\t      opts.retry.backoffCapMs,\n   398\t    );\n   399\t    const eligibleAt = new Date(Date.parse(opts.now) + backoffMs).toISOString();\n   400\t    db.exec(\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE;\");\n   401\t    try {\n   402\t      db.prepare(\n   403\t        `UPDATE work_item\n   404\t         SET status = 'queued', attempts = ?, last_error = ?,\n   405\t             claimed_at = NULL, claim_expires_at = NULL, eligible_at = ?\n   406\t         WHERE work_item_id = ?`,\n   407\t      ).run(attempts, opts.reason, eligibleAt, item.workItemId);\n   408\t      db.exec(\"COMMIT;\");\n   409\t    } catch (cause) {\n   410\t      db.exec(\"ROLLBACK;\");\n   411\t      throw cause;\n   412\t    }\n   413\t    return { terminal: false, attempts, eligibleAt };\n   414\t  }\n   415\t  failTerminal(db, item, { reason: opts.reason, formState: \"failed\", attempts, now: opts.now });\n   416\t  return { terminal: true, attempts };\n   417\t}\n   418\t\n   419\t// Cascade tidy (tech design issue 1): delete still-queued items for the given\n   420\t// (kind, sourceRef) targets, returning the deleted ids for the mutation\n   421\t// result's `superseded` list. Runs inside the caller's ambient transaction —\n   422\t// the cascade owns the BEGIN/COMMIT — so no transaction is opened here.\n   423\t// Claimed items are deliberately left alone: the source-version check (DD-3)\n   424\t// discards their stale completions. Ships here as mechanics; its only caller\n   425\t// is Story 5's cascade.\n   426\texport function supersedeQueued(\n   427\t  db: DatabaseSync,\n   428\t  targets: ReadonlyArray<{ kind: WorkKind; sourceRef: WorkSourceRef }>,\n   429\t): string[] {\n   430\t  const ids: string[] = [];\n   431\t  const remove = db.prepare(\n   432\t    `DELETE FROM work_item WHERE status = 'queued' AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\n   433\t     RETURNING work_item_id`,\n   434\t  );\n   435\t  for (const target of targets) {\n   436\t    const rows = remove.all(target.kind, JSON.stringify(target.sourceRef)) as unknown as Array<{\n   437\t      work_item_id: string;\n   438\t    }>;\n   439\t    for (const row of rows) ids.push(row.work_item_id);\n   440\t  }\n   441\t  return ids;\n   442\t}\n   443\t\n   444\t// The requeue no-op rule's EXISTS check (AC-4.5): is there a live item —\n   445\t// queued or in flight — for this kind and source at this source version?\n   446\t// Runs inside the caller's requeue transaction so the check and the enqueue\n   447\t// commit together (anti-shim: a check-then-enqueue split across transactions\n   448\t// reintroduces the duplicate-work race). Version-scoped on purpose: a\n   449\t// claimed pre-mutation item at an older version is dying work whose\n   450\t// completion DD-3 will discard — it must not block the repair.\n   451\texport function hasLiveItem(\n   452\t  db: DatabaseSync,\n   453\t  kind: WorkKind,\n   454\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef,\n   455\t  sourceVersion: number,\n   456\t): boolean {\n   457\t  const row = db\n   458\t    .prepare(\n   459\t      `SELECT 1 FROM work_item\n   460\t       WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed') AND kind = ? AND source_ref = ?\n   461\t         AND COALESCE(json_extract(payload, '$.sourceVersion'), 1) = ?\n   462\t       LIMIT 1`,\n   463\t    )\n   464\t    .get(kind, JSON.stringify(sourceRef), sourceVersion);\n   465\t  return row !== undefined;\n   466\t}\n   467\t\n   468\t// Live items left in the queue — the drain report's `remaining` and the\n   469\t// repair report's join source (Story 4).\n   470\texport function countLiveItems(db: DatabaseSync): number {\n   471\t  const row = db\n   472\t    .prepare(`SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM work_item WHERE status IN ('queued', 'claimed')`)\n   473\t    .get() as unknown as { n: number | bigint };\n   474\t  return Number(row.n);\n   475\t}\n   476\t\n   477\texport interface QueueDetailRow {\n   478\t  workItemId: string;\n   479\t  owner: WorkOwner;\n   480\t  kind: string;\n   481\t  sourceRef: WorkSourceRef;\n   482\t  status: \"queued\" | \"claimed\";\n   483\t  attempts: number;\n   484\t  lastError?: string;\n   485\t  eligibleAt?: string;\n   486\t  claimedAt?: string;\n   487\t  claimExpiresAt?: string;\n   488\t  queuedAt: string;\n   489\t  sourceVersion: number;\n   490\t}\n   491\t\n   492\t// Mechanical detail of every live row in queue order — what the repair\n   493\t// report joins against derivation (Flow 4) and what tests read to assert\n   494\t// lease/backoff state without raw SQL at every call site.\n   495\texport function queueDetail(db: DatabaseSync): QueueDetailRow[] {\n   496\t  const rows = db\n   497\t    .prepare(\n   498\t      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, status, attempts, last_error,\n   499\t              eligible_at, claimed_at, claim_expires_at, queued_at, payload\n   500\t       FROM work_item ORDER BY rowid`,\n   501\t    )\n   502\t    .all() as unknown as Array<\n   503\t    RawClaimRow & {\n   504\t      status: string;\n   505\t      last_error: string | null;\n   506\t      eligible_at: string | null;\n   507\t      claimed_at: string | null;\n   508\t      claim_expires_at: string | null;\n   509\t    }\n   510\t  >;\n   511\t  return rows.map((row) => {\n   512\t    const item = toClaimedItem(row);\n   513\t    const detail: QueueDetailRow = {\n   514\t      workItemId: item.workItemId,\n   515\t      owner: item.owner,\n   516\t      kind: item.kind,\n   517\t      sourceRef: item.sourceRef,\n   518\t      status: row.status as \"queued\" | \"claimed\",\n   519\t      attempts: item.attempts,\n   520\t      queuedAt: item.queuedAt,\n   521\t      sourceVersion: item.sourceVersion,\n   522\t    };\n   523\t    if (row.last_error !== null) detail.lastError = row.last_error;\n   524\t    if (row.eligible_at !== null) detail.eligibleAt = row.eligible_at;\n   525\t    if (row.claimed_at !== null) detail.claimedAt = row.claimed_at;\n   526\t    if (row.claim_expires_at !== null) detail.claimExpiresAt = row.claim_expires_at;\n   527\t    return detail;\n   528\t  });\n   529\t}\n   530\t\n   531\t// Listing in queue order (insertion order within the walk); each owning\n   532\t// domain lists only its own items.\n   533\texport function listItems(db: DatabaseSync, owner: WorkOwner): WorkItemRecord[] {\n   534\t  const rows = db\n   535\t    .prepare(\n   536\t      `SELECT work_item_id, owner, kind, source_ref, queued_at\n   537\t       FROM work_item WHERE owner = ? ORDER BY rowid`,\n   538\t    )\n   539\t    .all(owner) as unknown as RawWorkItemRow[];\n   540\t  return rows.map((row) => ({\n   541\t    workItemId: row.work_item_id,\n   542\t    owner: row.owner as WorkOwner,\n   543\t    kind: row.kind as WorkKind,\n   544\t    sourceRef: JSON.parse(row.source_ref) as WorkSourceRef,\n   545\t    status: \"queued\",\n   546\t    queuedAt: row.queued_at,\n   547\t  }));\n   548\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"artifactsRead\":[\"docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/stories/00-foundation.md\",\"docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/tech-design.md\",\"docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/test-plan.md\",\"docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/artifacts/00-foundation/006-implementor.json\",\"packages/lhc/src/shared/derivation.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/shared/storage.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/shared/context.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/internal/create.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/logging/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/sdk.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/derive.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/compose.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/shared/view.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/shared/inspect.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/render.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/select.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/thread-migration.test.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/fixtures/threads.ts\"],\"reviewScopeSummary\":\"Initial verification of Story 0 against AC-5.1 through AC-5.5, the derivation vocabulary rename DoD, v7 migration, public SDK logging surface, logging tests, and production fallback/logging paths.\",\"priorFindingStatuses\":[],\"newFindings\":[{\"id\":\"S0-F1\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Ready derivation rows still persist fallback markers instead of logging fallback events only\",\"evidence\":\"AC-5.3 requires a fallback event to exist only in the log and says ready derivation output must carry no degraded/fallback marker (00-foundation.md:43-48). Production turn derivation still collects fallback gaps and attaches them to ready turn_rendering writes (packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/derive.ts:83,95,106-115). Chunk summaries do the same (packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/derive.ts:164-175,192-200). The queue completion path persists those gaps on ready derivation rows (packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:304-312). `rg writeLog` found no production fallback call sites outside the SDK wrapper and logging tests, and TC-5.3a only manually inserts a clean derivation row before writing a log (packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts:93-143).\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/derive.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/derivation-turns.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-5.3\",\"TC-5.3a\",\"DD-5\",\"Story 0 DoD: no fallback marker on ready derivation outputs\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"blocking\":true},{\"id\":\"S0-F2\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"writeLog participates in the caller transaction\",\"evidence\":\"The story technical notes require `writeLog` to be fail-soft and not share the caller transaction (00-foundation.md:133-134), and DD-5 says it must not share the caller's transaction (tech-design.md:127-129). The implementation inserts through `ctx.db` directly (packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/logging/index.ts:43-59). A focused rollback probe wrote a log inside `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, rolled back the caller transaction, and observed `{\\\"n\\\":0}` log rows; an independent log transaction would have left one row. TC-5.5a only injects a fake throwing prepare function, so it does not prove real-store transaction isolation (packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts:220-235).\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/logging/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-5.5\",\"TC-5.5a\",\"DD-5\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":true},{\"id\":\"S0-F3\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Derivation vocabulary rename is not complete across current production/public shapes\",\"evidence\":\"Story 0 scope and DoD require the form vocabulary to be renamed across code and schema (00-foundation.md:15-18,162), and DD-1 says every reference changes from form to derivation vocabulary (tech-design.md:87-89). Current shared/public view and inspect shapes still expose `formUsed` and `formCounts` (packages/lhc/src/shared/view.ts:116-125,152; packages/lhc/src/shared/inspect.ts:57,74). The compact production path persists those names into thread_view JSON (packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/index.ts:458-479,508-514), and thread-view internals still define `FormSnapshot`/`FormLookup` for derivation state rows (packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/render.ts:131-145).\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/shared/view.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/shared/inspect.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/render.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/select.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"Story 0 Scope In: derivation vocabulary rename\",\"DD-1\",\"Story 0 DoD: rename complete across schema, types, work-queue kinds, and tests\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"blocking\":true}],\"openFindings\":[{\"id\":\"S0-F1\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Ready derivation rows still persist fallback markers instead of logging fallback events only\",\"evidence\":\"AC-5.3 requires a fallback event to exist only in the log and says ready derivation output must carry no degraded/fallback marker (00-foundation.md:43-48). Production turn derivation still collects fallback gaps and attaches them to ready turn_rendering writes (packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/derive.ts:83,95,106-115). Chunk summaries do the same (packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/derive.ts:164-175,192-200). The queue completion path persists those gaps on ready derivation rows (packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:304-312). `rg writeLog` found no production fallback call sites outside the SDK wrapper and logging tests, and TC-5.3a only manually inserts a clean derivation row before writing a log (packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts:93-143).\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/derive.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/derivation-turns.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-5.3\",\"TC-5.3a\",\"DD-5\",\"Story 0 DoD: no fallback marker on ready derivation outputs\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"blocking\":true},{\"id\":\"S0-F2\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"writeLog participates in the caller transaction\",\"evidence\":\"The story technical notes require `writeLog` to be fail-soft and not share the caller transaction (00-foundation.md:133-134), and DD-5 says it must not share the caller's transaction (tech-design.md:127-129). The implementation inserts through `ctx.db` directly (packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/logging/index.ts:43-59). A focused rollback probe wrote a log inside `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, rolled back the caller transaction, and observed `{\\\"n\\\":0}` log rows; an independent log transaction would have left one row. TC-5.5a only injects a fake throwing prepare function, so it does not prove real-store transaction isolation (packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts:220-235).\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/logging/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/logging-surface.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-5.5\",\"TC-5.5a\",\"DD-5\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":true},{\"id\":\"S0-F3\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Derivation vocabulary rename is not complete across current production/public shapes\",\"evidence\":\"Story 0 scope and DoD require the form vocabulary to be renamed across code and schema (00-foundation.md:15-18,162), and DD-1 says every reference changes from form to derivation vocabulary (tech-design.md:87-89). Current shared/public view and inspect shapes still expose `formUsed` and `formCounts` (packages/lhc/src/shared/view.ts:116-125,152; packages/lhc/src/shared/inspect.ts:57,74). The compact production path persists those names into thread_view JSON (packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/index.ts:458-479,508-514), and thread-view internals still define `FormSnapshot`/`FormLookup` for derivation state rows (packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/render.ts:131-145).\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/shared/view.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/shared/inspect.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/render.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/select.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"Story 0 Scope In: derivation vocabulary rename\",\"DD-1\",\"Story 0 DoD: rename complete across schema, types, work-queue kinds, and tests\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"blocking\":true}],\"requirementCoverage\":{\"verified\":[\"AC-5.1 / TC-5.1a: info, warning, and error log levels are persisted by the SDK logging path and covered by logging-surface.test.ts.\",\"AC-5.2 / TC-5.2a: internal writeLog and SDK instance logging.write write to the same durable log table in logging-surface.test.ts.\",\"AC-5.3 / TC-5.3b: failed derivation state and reason remain on the derivation row independent of log entries in logging-surface.test.ts.\",\"AC-5.4 / TC-5.4a: queryLog supports level, derivationType, subjectId, and reason filters; logging-surface.test.ts covers mixed-field filtering.\",\"V7 schema migration is registered and story/epic gates pass under user_version 7.\"],\"unverified\":[\"AC-5.3 / TC-5.3a production path: actual fallback during derivation construction still writes gaps to ready derivation rows and has no production log call site.\",\"AC-5.5 / TC-5.5a transaction containment: writeLog catches write failures, but a real log write shares and rolls back with the caller transaction.\",\"Story 0 derivation vocabulary rename DoD: public/shared thread-view shapes and persisted JSON still use formUsed/formCounts/FormSnapshot vocabulary.\"]},\"gatesRun\":[{\"command\":\"cd packages/lhc && pnpm exec vitest run test/logging-surface.test.ts\",\"result\":\"pass\"},{\"command\":\"cd packages/lhc && pnpm run verify\",\"result\":\"pass\"},{\"command\":\"cd packages/lhc && pnpm run verify-all\",\"result\":\"pass\"},{\"command\":\"cd packages/lhc && pnpm exec tsx -e '<writeLog inside BEGIN IMMEDIATE, then ROLLBACK; assert log count === 1>'\",\"result\":\"fail\"}],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"Ready derivation completion still persists fallback `gaps` on derivation rows, so fallback markers remain in the derivation state/output channel instead of only the log.\",\"writeLog uses the caller's DatabaseSync handle and participates in the caller transaction; rollback proof observed zero persisted log rows after caller rollback.\",\"Thread-view compact still persists form vocabulary in arrangement/source-state JSON (`formUsed`, `formCounts`) and exposes it through shared view/inspect surfaces.\"],\"recommendedNextStep\":\"revise\",\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"openQuestions\":[],\"additionalObservations\":[\"The configured story and epic gates passed, but they do not cover the rollback isolation proof or production fallback-channel behavior.\",\"`verify` and `verify-all` reported 9 skipped real-inference tests because LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY was unset; I did not use those skipped tests as Story 0 evidence.\"]}"}}
